WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:04.210
Film Courage: Can we think of a film that
has done quite well where if the character

00:00:04.210 --> 00:00:09.820
hadn’t been so interesting or their transformation
so stark…that if you hadn’t plugged in

00:00:09.820 --> 00:00:11.580
that character, the story would be no good.

00:00:11.580 --> 00:00:13.660
It was too simple or the story didn’t really
work.

00:00:13.660 --> 00:00:17.740
But because the character’s journey was
so profound, it resonated with people?

00:00:17.740 --> 00:00:18.740
Scott Myers, DePaul University Professor/Screenwriter/Creator
Gointothestory.blcklst(dot)com: I think it’s

00:00:18.740 --> 00:00:20.590
like almost any story.

00:00:20.590 --> 00:00:30.900
I mean if the protagonist isn’t compelling,
if we don’t have an emotional connection

00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:34.579
to them, then what is the point right?

00:00:34.579 --> 00:00:36.280
Like I’m a huge Pixar fan.

00:00:36.280 --> 00:00:43.030
I’ve got sons that are 9 1/2 years apart
and came along at a time where I was basically

00:00:43.030 --> 00:00:45.780
forced to see every Pixar movie.

00:00:45.780 --> 00:00:50.400
I say forced because they came out and the
kids wanted to see them which was just a wonderful

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:55.070
blessing for me because Pixar is just phenomenal
storytellers.

00:00:55.070 --> 00:01:00.079
And I know because I know Mary Coleman who
is the head of the story department there

00:01:00.079 --> 00:01:08.299
at Pixar that nothing gets green lit there
unless they know clearly what those points

00:01:08.299 --> 00:01:11.539
of universal connection there are to the audience.

00:01:11.539 --> 00:01:17.679
How is the audience going to tie into the
story emotionally?

00:01:17.679 --> 00:01:26.450
So I would say that any great movie or any
great story, that traffics in that.

00:01:26.450 --> 00:01:35.219
I mean there has got to be something going
on even if it’s an anti-hero movie where

00:01:35.219 --> 00:01:36.639
there is something human there.

00:01:36.639 --> 00:01:41.340
Where we can connect with, that we can relate
to.

00:01:41.340 --> 00:01:45.529
TAXI DRIVER for example (Travis Bickle played
by Robert De Niro).

00:01:45.529 --> 00:01:50.289
Here’s a guy who is lonely.

00:01:50.289 --> 00:01:53.159
He wants to go on a date.

00:01:53.159 --> 00:01:58.919
He is living…it’s just loneliness right
there.

00:01:58.919 --> 00:02:05.889
Everybody at some point has been lonely or
felt like an outsider or an outcast and so

00:02:05.889 --> 00:02:12.180
that is enough right there that I’m interested
in the guy to see how he is existing in one

00:02:12.180 --> 00:02:18.129
of the most heavily populated places in the
United States (Manhattan, New York).

00:02:18.129 --> 00:02:20.220
So I would say that is absolutely critical.

00:02:20.220 --> 00:02:22.730
You’ve got to find that point of emotional
connection.

00:02:22.730 --> 00:02:25.510
I’d say it even goes further than that.

00:02:25.510 --> 00:02:32.470
You as a writer have to find how you are emotionally
connected to the character because that is

00:02:32.470 --> 00:02:38.681
the kind of passion that can manifest itself
on the page where the words lift up off of

00:02:38.681 --> 00:02:45.299
the page and into the imagination of the reader
because you are so dialed into that character

00:02:45.299 --> 00:02:49.069
that their experience infuses your own writing.

00:02:49.069 --> 00:02:51.579
So it is absolutely critical.

00:02:51.579 --> 00:02:54.849
People go to movies to think yes, be entertained
yes.

00:02:54.849 --> 00:02:58.459
But I think fundamentally they go to movies
to feel something.

00:02:58.459 --> 00:03:04.790
Film Courage: Well if we take slightly older
movies, ones that had Humphrey Bogart (THE

00:03:04.790 --> 00:03:10.269
MALTESE FALCON, CASABLANCA, etc.), how do
we feel a connection even though we don’t

00:03:10.269 --> 00:03:12.590
see ourselves as his character type?

00:03:12.590 --> 00:03:14.280
His characters always had lots of confidence.

00:03:14.280 --> 00:03:18.950
Maybe they were a little more jaded or cynical
(they were always the truth tellers).

00:03:18.950 --> 00:03:21.970
How are we tapping into those same Humphrey
Bogart characters.

00:03:21.970 --> 00:03:27.230
Scott: Okay so let’s take CASABLANCA which
is the number one script on the WGA [Writers

00:03:27.230 --> 00:03:31.870
Guild of America] Top 101 Greatest Screenplays
which is ironic because they never finished

00:03:31.870 --> 00:03:32.870
the script.

00:03:32.870 --> 00:03:34.989
They never really had a complete script.

00:03:34.989 --> 00:03:38.319
But what is his issue [Rick Blaine played
Humphrey Bogart]?

00:03:38.319 --> 00:03:41.499
He is in a state of disunity at the beginning.

00:03:41.499 --> 00:03:42.819
He started off as a loyalist.

00:03:42.819 --> 00:03:46.209
He was a passionate believer in good and the
right.

00:03:46.209 --> 00:03:51.700
He fought for the side of the loyalists in
the Spanish Civil War.

00:03:51.700 --> 00:03:52.973
He falls in love with Ilsa [played by Ingrid
Bergman].

00:03:52.973 --> 00:03:56.939
They have this wonderful ideals experience
in Paris.

00:03:56.939 --> 00:03:59.390
Then the Germans are coming into town.

00:03:59.390 --> 00:04:02.749
He goes to the train station, she doesn’t
show up.

00:04:02.749 --> 00:04:05.220
His heart is broken.

00:04:05.220 --> 00:04:09.010
So he goes to Casablanca and starts this business.

00:04:09.010 --> 00:04:10.599
He drinks alone.

00:04:10.599 --> 00:04:12.379
He plays chess alone.

00:04:12.379 --> 00:04:18.310
He does have some sort of romantic dalliance
with that one woman but there is no real connection

00:04:18.310 --> 00:04:19.560
there.

00:04:19.560 --> 00:04:21.769
And he’s a complete cynic at this point.

00:04:21.769 --> 00:04:28.000
He has cut himself off from that idealistic
part of himself that used to exist.

00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:32.530
So that whole hard-boiled Humphrey Bogart
“I came for the waters.

00:04:32.530 --> 00:04:33.530
I was misinformed.”

00:04:33.530 --> 00:04:34.729
That whole thing right?

00:04:34.729 --> 00:04:40.870
That’s his mask, that is his cynical mask,
that is his public persona.

00:04:40.870 --> 00:04:44.310
Inside is his idealism, is his passion for
life.

00:04:44.310 --> 00:04:45.960
It’s still there but it’s buried.

00:04:45.960 --> 00:04:51.020
Per [Carl] Jung it’s in the darkness of
the unconscious, it needs to come out.

00:04:51.020 --> 00:04:53.920
Which is why it happens outside as fate.

00:04:53.920 --> 00:04:55.340
Who shows up and where?

00:04:55.340 --> 00:04:56.340
Ilsa!

00:04:56.340 --> 00:04:59.740
And he says “Of all the lousy gin joints
in the world.”

00:04:59.740 --> 00:05:00.740
Right?

00:05:00.740 --> 00:05:03.259
Yes, it’s like the biggest coincidence in
the world or is it?

00:05:03.259 --> 00:05:04.259
It’s like fate.

00:05:04.259 --> 00:05:10.870
Fate determines that she shows up there because
he’s got to deal with his past.

00:05:10.870 --> 00:05:14.750
She is clearly the attractor character for
the protagonist right?

00:05:14.750 --> 00:05:17.900
And so how do we relate to that?

00:05:17.900 --> 00:05:19.449
How many of us have been jilted?

00:05:19.449 --> 00:05:23.940
How many of us have had our hearts broken
in a relationship?

00:05:23.940 --> 00:05:25.199
That’s pretty universal.

00:05:25.199 --> 00:05:29.120
I suspect that 90 percent of people have been
in a situation where they have had their hearts

00:05:29.120 --> 00:05:30.639
broken.

00:05:30.639 --> 00:05:33.220
We can relate to that with this guy.

00:05:33.220 --> 00:05:36.880
When you really boil it down on a human level,
that’s what this guy is dealing with.

00:05:36.880 --> 00:05:42.240
For all that bluster and all that business
savvy and everything else and that cynicism,

00:05:42.240 --> 00:05:44.690
he’s a guy who had his heart broken.

00:05:44.690 --> 00:05:47.520
So there is a universal point of connection.

00:05:47.520 --> 00:05:49.040
Then these set of characters show up.

00:05:49.040 --> 00:05:52.169
There is Major Strasser who represents the
Germans.

00:05:52.169 --> 00:05:54.980
He is the nemesis character.

00:05:54.980 --> 00:05:59.080
Because Rick’s got these letters of transit
and originally he’s thinking about taking

00:05:59.080 --> 00:06:05.330
he and Ilsa (once they connect) he’s going
to take her and off they go to Casablanca.

00:06:05.330 --> 00:06:08.440
But there’s the mentor figure, Victor Laszlo.

00:06:08.440 --> 00:06:11.430
Who knows that Rick has that idealistic side.

00:06:11.430 --> 00:06:15.639
There is a great conversation that they have
where Laszlo comes in from a meeting and he’s

00:06:15.639 --> 00:06:20.240
had his wrist injured and so Rick and he stand
at the bar.

00:06:20.240 --> 00:06:26.130
And Laszlo tells him as much, basically intimates
that he knows that this guy can come back

00:06:26.130 --> 00:06:30.020
and be on the side of the good side, on the
right.

00:06:30.020 --> 00:06:33.710
And you’ve got Captain Renault who is one
of the great tricksters of all time.

00:06:33.710 --> 00:06:36.780
“I’m shocked…shocked to find there is
gambling going on here?”

00:06:36.780 --> 00:06:39.920
He plays all sides for his own good.

00:06:39.920 --> 00:06:44.669
So in the end Rick does get in touch with
his idealism and he does reconnect with that

00:06:44.669 --> 00:06:49.099
part of himself because he moves from selfish
to selfless.

00:06:49.099 --> 00:06:56.790
He gives the letters of transit and Laszlo
and Ilsa go off and he is contributing to

00:06:56.790 --> 00:06:59.069
the side of the good.

00:06:59.069 --> 00:07:04.300
But the main point (circling back to your
point), we related to him because he’s had

00:07:04.300 --> 00:07:06.729
his heart broken and that’s a universal
thing.

00:07:06.729 --> 00:07:09.289
So even thought it’s Sam Spade [protagonist
of Dashiell Hammett's 30’s novel, The Maltese

00:07:09.289 --> 00:07:14.840
Falcon] in effect there in CASABLANCA, he’s
still a guy who has been crushed romantically

00:07:14.840 --> 00:07:15.710
and emotionally.

